Cloud SQL — managed open-source.

Cloud SQL is the managed-database product for the three engines most production workloads already use. PostgreSQL, MySQL, and SQL Server. You don't pick a custom GCP query engine; you pick the open-source engine you'd run anyway and let GCP handle the operating system, patching, backups, replication, and the long tail of database operations.

The trade-off is exactly the one pa-ass asks of you in notebook four. You lose root on the V-M and gain freedom from operating it. For ninety percent of production O-L-T-P workloads, that's the right trade. The remaining ten percent — extreme scale, multi-region writes, very specific tuning — show up later in this chapter as AlloyDB, or in notebook nine as Spanner.

Three engines, one managed surface. PostgreSQL versions thirteen through seventeen. The modern default — strong feature set, well-tooled, good extension surface. MySQL five point seven, eight point zero, and eight point four. Pick when you have existing MySQL workloads or a strong MySQL preference. Less ambitious than Postgres for new work in twenty twenty-six. And SQL Server twenty seventeen, twenty nineteen, twenty twenty-two — Standard, Enterprise, Express, and Web editions. Pick when licensing or app-server compatibility forces it — bring-your-own-license Windows app stacks, Microsoft-shop migrations.

Machine shapes mirror Compute Engine's general-purpose families. You pick v-C-P-U and memory, and Cloud SQL provisions a V-M behind the scenes. Sizing rule: under-provision on C-P-U, over-provision on memory. Postgres and MySQL both benefit far more from R-A-M headroom than from extra cores.

High availability — regional Persistent Disk plus failover. Cloud SQL's high-availability shape is consistent across engines. The primary instance lives in one zone of a region. A standby lives in a second zone, kept in sync via regional Persistent Disk — synchronous block-level replication, from notebook five. On primary failure, Cloud SQL fails over to the standby in seconds, keeping the same connection endpoint.

This is a zone-failure high-availability story, not a region-failure one. For cross-region disaster recovery, you use cross-region read replicas that can be promoted to primary, with a manual promotion step. Notebook thirteen. For active-active multi-region writes you need Spanner or AlloyDB Omni. Cloud SQL doesn't do that.

Read replicas are async followers in the same region or another region. They handle read scaling for read-heavy workloads — B-I dashboards, search prefetch. They lag the primary by seconds normally, more under load. Never use a read replica for a query whose answer must reflect a write that just happened.

Connecting to Cloud SQL. Three ways to reach a Cloud SQL instance.

Public I-P plus authorized networks — the legacy default. Cloud SQL has a public endpoint; you allowlist source C-I-D-Rs. Acceptable for development, never the right answer in production.

Private I-P via Private Service Access, which is the legacy mechanism. Cloud SQL lives in a Google-owned V-P-C peered to yours over an allocated C-I-D-R. Most current production deployments still use this.

Private Service Connect, which is the modern mechanism. Cloud SQL publishes a P-S-C endpoint that you reach via an I-P in your own V-P-C. No C-I-D-R allocation, no peering. The right default for new instances.

The Cloud SQL Auth Proxy is the canonical Cloud SQL client. It runs as a sidecar on Cloud Run or GKE, or a process on Compute Engine. It authenticates to Cloud SQL using the workload's eye-ay-em identity — Workload Identity from notebook two, or the attached service account from notebook four. It establishes the T-L-S tunnel to the instance. And it exposes a local T-C-P socket your app connects to as one-two-seven dot zero dot zero dot one port five-four-three-two.

With the Auth Proxy, you stop managing database connection passwords for service accounts. The service account itself authenticates, gated by the role cloudsql-client. Combine with eye-ay-em database authentication to use the same service account for the in-database user, eliminating per-user passwords entirely.

Backups and maintenance. Automated backups run daily on a window you pick. They're stored in a Google-managed location and used for point-in-time recovery — P-I-T-R — with a seven-day default retention, configurable up to three-hundred-sixty-five days. P-I-T-R replays binary logs for MySQL or write-ahead logs for Postgres to restore to any second in the retention window.

Maintenance windows are when Cloud SQL applies engine and O-S patches. Pick a low-traffic window per instance. On high-availability instances, maintenance is applied to the standby first, then a failover, then the primary. Usually a few seconds of unavailability.

The boring operational ones — connection limits, eye-ay-em database auth bindings, the Postgres flag cloudsql dot iam-authentication. None of this is glamorous, all of it matters when a real incident hits.

AlloyDB — Postgres at GCP scale.

AlloyDB for PostgreSQL is a Postgres-compatible database engine that Google built on top of GCP's storage layer. It's wire-compatible with Postgres — your app drivers don't change — but the storage architecture and query engine are different, designed for O-L-T-P-plus-analytics workloads at scale that would stress Cloud SQL Postgres.

What makes AlloyDB different. Disaggregated storage. Compute — the Postgres-compatible engine — and storage — a multi-zone distributed log plus page service — are separated. You scale compute and storage independently. Failover is fast because storage isn't tied to a V-M.

Columnar engine. AlloyDB has an in-memory columnar accelerator for analytical queries. The same database serves O-L-T-P, row-oriented, and O-L-A-P, column-oriented workloads — H-T-A-P in marketing speak — without you running E-T-L to a separate analytics store.

Cross-region replicas. Async replicas in other regions for disaster recovery or geo-distributed reads.

Machine learning integration. Native model-inference functions in SQL, so you can call Vertex A-I models from a query.

Positioning versus Cloud SQL Postgres. Cloud SQL Postgres for standard O-L-T-P, sub-ten-terabyte databases, normal scale, normal cost. AlloyDB for large Postgres workloads — terabytes-plus — analytics-on-O-L-T-P without E-T-L, very high read throughput.

AlloyDB is more expensive than Cloud SQL Postgres at small sizes. It's optimised for the scale where Cloud SQL starts to strain.

AlloyDB Omni is the same engine packaged to run anywhere — your data center, AWS, Azure, on the developer's laptop in Docker. Used in hybrid architectures and for self-managed deployments where you want the AlloyDB engine without the GCP managed service.

Spanner is out of scope here, in scope in notebook nine. Spanner is GCP's globally distributed strong-consistency SQL database. It belongs in this chapter conceptually, but its shape — distributed storage, TrueTime, multi-region configurations — makes more sense alongside the other GCP-native scale databases like Bigtable and BigQuery in notebook nine.

The one-liner for now: use Spanner when you need horizontal write scale across regions with A-C-I-D transactions, and only then. It's the most powerful and most expensive option in this space.

Memorystore — managed in-memory.

When your relational database becomes the bottleneck, the next move is rarely "buy more database." It's "cache in front of the database." Memorystore is GCP's managed in-memory store, available in three flavours.

Three engines. Redis, with rich data types — lists, hashes, sorted sets, streams — plus Lua, transactions, and pub-sub. Default. Most workloads want Redis features. Memcached, which is simple, multi-threaded, and large-instance-fast. Use it for pure GET-SET caching at scale, no need for data types. And Valkey, the open-source Redis fork after the Redis license change. Use it for new deployments wanting the Redis A-P-I with open-source guarantees.

Memorystore for Redis has tiers. Basic — single node, no replication. For dev, test, or non-critical caches. Standard — primary plus replica in different zones, automatic failover. Production default. And Memorystore for Redis Cluster — sharded, multi-node, horizontal scale. For very large caches that don't fit one node.

Networking. Memorystore lives in a Google-managed V-P-C accessed via Private Service Access, the legacy mechanism, or Private Service Connect, the modern mechanism. Same connectivity model as Cloud SQL.

Cache patterns. Lazy loading, also called cache-aside. Application code checks the cache first. On miss, reads the database and writes the result back to the cache. Pros: cache stays cold for never-read data. Cons: every miss is a database hit, which can stampede during traffic spikes.

Write-through. Every database write also writes the corresponding cache entry. Pros: cache is always fresh. Cons: more write traffic to the cache, cache pollution for data that's rarely read.

Write-behind, also called write-back. Writes go to the cache first, asynchronously flushed to the database. Pros: extremely fast writes. Cons: cache failure can lose data — only safe with replicated caches and the ability to re-derive lost writes.

T-T-L-based expiry. Every cache entry has a time-to-live. Expired entries are re-fetched. Pros: bounded staleness without explicit invalidation. Cons: stale data within the T-T-L window.

Common patterns by use case. Sessions — lazy load with T-T-L matching session length. Redis hashes are the canonical store. Leaderboards — Redis sorted sets, computed in cache only. The database doesn't need to know. Hot reads — lazy load with T-T-L around sixty seconds on highly-queried records, like the homepage or product detail page. And counters — Redis I-N-C-R, periodically flushed to the database via a background job.

The cache is not the database. Anything that only exists in cache must be recomputable from the database. Otherwise an outage loses data.

A short decision tree to close out. The hard questions are mostly about transactional shape and scale.

Standard O-L-T-P, single-region, under a few terabytes: Cloud SQL. Postgres if you're picking fresh, MySQL if existing, SQL Server if licensing forces it.

Postgres-shaped, but tens of terabytes or H-T-A-P-style workload: AlloyDB for PostgreSQL.

Globally distributed writes, strong consistency, A-C-I-D: Spanner — notebook nine.

Anything-key-value, schemaless, document-oriented, real-time, or scale beyond SQL: also notebook nine. Firestore, Bigtable, BigQuery.

Add caching when database hits are repeated, not as the first move: Memorystore.

Three anti-patterns to avoid. "We need Spanner because we want global redundancy" — Cloud SQL with cross-region replicas covers most disaster recovery. Spanner is for horizontal write scale; it's overkill and expensive for high availability alone. "We'll start on Cloud SQL and migrate to Spanner later" — Spanner's schema model and lack of certain Postgres features, like extensions, triggers, and certain index types, make migration non-trivial. Pick deliberately at design time. And "Memcached is faster than Redis" — Memcached has been faster for pure-string GET-SET at very high queries-per-second historically. For most workloads, Redis's data types and persistence options earn their cost. Pick Memcached only when you've measured and need it.

What carries into later chapters. Cloud SQL and AlloyDB sit behind Cloud Run services from notebook four via the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy or P-S-C. Memorystore sits in the same V-P-C and is reached via private I-P. Notebook nine picks up NoSQL and analytics, and goes deep on Spanner — the database deferred from this chapter. Notebook thirteen covers backup, point-in-time recovery, and cross-region disaster recovery for these stores.

Three habits to carry forward. Private I-P or P-S-C over public I-P for any production database — public-I-P databases are a security regression. Workload Identity plus eye-ay-em database authentication — no database passwords in app config. And cache aggressively, but never as the only copy of state — a cache outage should degrade performance, not lose data.
